cial is again  we do lots of enrichment for these  especially these all the animals here we  want to keep them not just physically  healthy but also mentally healthy so  it's one of the reasons why we move the  Bears in and out of the habitat all day  long and different groupings we can we  change that schedule every day so they  never really know what's happening every  time they come out here we've put all  kinds of interesting things out here we  bury lots of fruits and vegetables under  those rocks and logs we hang stuff in  the trees we give them coolers to test  which is important for testing the  coolers but also it gives the bears some  enrichment there are some fish in the  pond right now I don't think they know  that yet but we do lots of things to  give them enrichment so that's one of  the things we're really proud about  proud of here at the center is the way  that we care for these animals  absolutely so you talked a little bit  about the bears here and what the  typical day might look like for them  yeah so a typical day for these bears  again we're gonna move them in and out  of this habitat area bears in the wild  are not active all the time they're  mostly active in the morning and the  evenings they'll actually spend a lot of  the day in what we call a day bed that  is napping yeah he's he's I don't know  if he's trying really hard to get into  it or just playing with that in the  water but regardless it is fun to watch  so so there's in the wild again they  spend a lot of their days napping  resting they want to conserve their  energy they don't want to expend a lot  of energy theirs are omnivores and we  call them opportunistic omnivores  they're always looking for the easiest  food source with the least caloric  expansion so again they rest a lot in  the wild so here at the center we mimic  a lot of what they would do in the wild  so when they're out here they are more  active they can forage for food very  they can again you know sometimes we 